05/13/24,Mortgage,Conventional home mortgage,Applying for a mortgage or refinancing an existing mortgage,Confusing or misleading advertising or marketing,"My mortgage was bought by a debt collector XXXX XXXX, from my original mortgage company Veterans United Home Loans. I never received a letter from XXXX about them buying my mortgage, and asked them to validate the debt, to show that I actually owed the debt after they purchased it. I asked for the original copy of the promissory note in a notarized letter to both mortgage companies, and they both only sent me a copy of the promissory note, that I and my wife signed at closing on XX/XX/XXXX. I did some studying and found that banks do not loan their assets, so there was no loan. The last Promissory note according to my research, is monetized as per title XXXX1 USC 5118 ( d ) ( 2 ) ,31 USCA 463 and public Law 97-258 ( XX/XX/XXXX. And in accordance with 31 USC 5103 and USC 8, such promissory notes are national Bank currency and thereby coin or currency of the United States, by rule of law, US Supreme Court, and private BAR association statutory Bylaws legal definition. Also 12 USC 1813. These laws clearly state that the Promissory note is monetized or in a lot of cases invested to make money with risk. Giving the above laws to both Veterans United Home Loans and XXXX XXXX, and asking them to verify or validate the debt has been without results, leaving me to where I am now. My last conversation with XXXX XXXX, was with a gentleman who said yes my debt was purchased by XXXX XXXX and that I was sent paperwork to validate the debt.i explained to him that there was no loan, I never signed a check or received a bank transfer to my bank account with the money to purchase my home, so where did the money come from? He just was silent on the other end of the phone and never gave me an answer. When I wrote to Veterans United Home Loans, and to XXXX XXXX, I included all the laws to back up my claim to not receiving a loan such as : 12 USC 83, 15 USC 1601,1602,1611, 1604, and 15 USC 1635. To this day, I have not received the debt validation I asked for and did not pay my mortgage as I told them I wasn't, until the debt was validated. My wife and I are arguing because of this and I would truly appreciate your help in resolving this issue. Thank you for your time.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"Mortgage Research Center, LLC",NY,131XX,Servicemember,Consent provided,Web,05/13/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8994028
