03/06/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Account information incorrect,"According to the Fair Credit Reporting Act, the accounts listed below have breached my federally protected consumer rights on accurate credit reporting. 

XXXX XXXX # XXXX is in violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act by inaccurately reporting information to XXXX, Equifax, and XXXX. Upon reviewing my credit report, I observed discrepancies in the last activity dates- XX/XX/XXXX, with XXXX, XX/XX/XXXX, with Equifax, and XX/XX/XXXX with XXXX. Furthermore, XXXX  XXXX has Inconsistencies in my payment history across the three bureaus, which contravene the Federal Credit Reporting Act. For Instance, XXXX  XXXX, reported a timely payment on XX/XX/XXXX, to Equifax but failed to provide any record for the same date on XXXX and XXXX. Similarly, the reported on-time payments for XXXX to XX/XX/XXXX to Equifax but XXXX this information from XXXX and XXXX. Additionally, XXXX XXXX 's payment history percentages differ across bureaus- claiming a XXXX percent payment history for Equifax XXXX while indicating XXXX percent for XXXX AND XXXX Percent for XXXX. These inconsistencies require immediate correction to align with the accurate reporting standards mandated by the Fair Credit Reporting Act. I would like this removed totally from my account. 

15 U.S. Code 1681 ( a ) ( 1 ) The banking system is dependent upon fair and accurate credit reporting. Inaccurate credit reports directly impair the efficiency of the banking system, and unfair credit reporting methods undermine the public confidence which is essential to the continued functioning of the banking system.

15 U.S. Code 1681 ( b ) It is the purpose of this subchapter to require that consumer reporting agencies adopt reasonable procedures for meeting the needs of commerce for consumer credit, personnel, insurance, and other information in a manner which is fair and equitable to the consumer, with regard to the confidentiality, accuracy, relevancy, and proper utilization of such information in accordance with the requirements of this subchapter.

Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) ( 15 U.S.C. 1681 and following ), you may sue a credit reporting agency for negligent or willful noncompliance with the law within two years after you discover the harmful behavior or within five years after the harmful behavior occurs, whichever is sooner.",None,"EQUIFAX, INC.",NY,10562,None,Consent provided,Web,03/06/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8487445
