08/23/24,Checking or savings account,Checking account,Managing an account,Problem using a debit or ATM card,This company ( ROBINHOOD ) withdraw fun a more of two occasions without my permission or authorization..I call the bank to stop and refund me the money back and they do it again using others alias like ( ROBINHOOD Market ) first and now ( ROBINHOOD Debit ) .. ( {$1000.00} )... so they just won't stop ... withdrawing money any way they can ... so I want to stop this fraud by this company ... thank you,None,ROBINHOOD MARKETS INC.,NY,10457,Older American,Consent provided,Web,08/23/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,9899113
09/13/24,Debt or credit management,Credit repair services,Didn't provide services promised,None,"I am writing to formally express my dissatisfaction with Tally, a credit servicing company I had an agreement with, which has since filed for bankruptcy. When I initially signed up for Tally, the understanding was that it would provide me with a line of credit to help manage my credit card debt by paying off my balances and thereby reducing my interest rates.

However, following the company 's bankruptcy, my situation has drastically changed, and I am now being held to a fixed loan, which was never the agreed-upon purpose of the service. I never consented to a traditional loan with Tally, and the product was always intended to be a flexible line of credit for reducing credit card interestnot a fixed loan. This change has placed an unexpected and unfair burden on me financially. 

I did not agree to this shift in loan terms, and it feels misleading and unjust. I am deeply concerned about how this has been handled and the lack of communication and transparency regarding the companys bankruptcy and the sale of my account to another lender. I believe I have been misled by Tallys initial service offerings and how they have been altered without my consent. 

I request immediate clarification regarding : 1. How my loan terms were changed and why I was not consulted.

2. The legal justification for converting my flexible line of credit into a fixed loan.

3. What steps are being taken to address the concerns of customers affected by Tallys bankruptcy.

