03/17/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"REMOVE ALL INACCURATE PERSONAL INFORMATION, NON-MAILABLE ADDRESSES, DUPLICATE PHONE NUMBERS, INACTIVE EMAILS, ALL TRANSACTS HISTORY, AND ALL UNLAWFUL ACCOUNT STATUS POSTING. 
XXXX XXXX XXXX ( TRUE AND CORRECT ) any other variation is incorrect and not a true representation of me as a consumer and has been submitted falsely and nefariously and collected and shared without my knowledge or consent!!! 
XXXX XXXX XXXX. 
XXXX. XXXX XXXX, New York XXXX ( TRUE AND CORRECT ) all other addresses are non-mailable, and have been submitted nefariously and with malicious intent, as a consumer, I do not send, receive, or affiliate with any other address. Various other phone numbers have been Maliciously submitted in error, and further collected and shared without my knowledge or consent. XXXX ( TRUE AND CORRECT ) I have had the same phone number for many years, the other phone numbers listed on consumer reports are incorrect and submitted to financial institutions without my knowledge. They have been collected and shared in connection to me in error. I can not be reached through any of these lines, yet they appear on my consumer report seemingly nefarious, as they in no way identify a True and Correct representation of me as a consumer and are not a valid means of communication to me as a consumer ; and should be removed. 

As a federally protected consumer under the FCRA, I have the right to ensure that my private information is not shared and kept secure and confidential. XXXX, Equifax, and XXXX are consumer reporting agencies who are doing the complete opposite, with their malicious treatment of my nonpublic personal information. They have failed to exercise their duties according to the standards set forth by Congress. 15 U.S. Code 1681 ( 4 ) states that There is a need to insure that consumer reporting agencies exercise their grave responsibilities with fairness, impartiality, and a respect for the consumers right to privacy. My right to these protections is further backed by Congresss policy found in 15 U.S. Code 6801, which obligates financial institutions to respect its customers privacy by ensuring its customers nonpublic personal is kept confidential. 15 U.S. Code 6801 ( a ) Privacy Obligation Policy states It is the policy of the Congress that each financial institution has an affirmative and continuing obligation to respect the privacy of its customers and to protect the security and confidentiality of those customers nonpublic personal information.

15 U.S. Code 6827 - Definitions ( B ) Certain financial institutions specifically included - The term financial institution includes any depository institution ( as defined in section 461 ( b ) ( 1 ) ( A ) of title 12 ), any broker or dealer, any investment adviser or investment company, any insurance company, any loan or finance company, any credit card issuer or operator of a credit card system, and any consumer reporting agency that compiles and maintains files on consumers on a nationwide basis ( as defined in section 1681a ( p ) of this title ). It is quite clear that XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX are a financial institution by this definition under 15 U.S. Code 6801. Congress has established strict guidelines that prevent a consumer reporting agency from furnishing a consumer report without the written instructions from the consumer to whom it relates. Subsection 604 Permissible purposes of consumer reports [ 1681b ] ( a ) In general. States Subject to subsection ( c ), any consumer reporting agency may furnish a consumer report under the following circumstances and no other : ( 2 ). in accordance with the written instructions of the consumer to whom it relates. XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX the financial institutions and XXXX, EQUIFAX, & XXXX the consumer reporting agencies do not have my consent or authorization to furnish this information and they most certainly do not have my written consent. Per the FCRA, as a federally protected consumer, I hereby make void, cancel, and revoke any and all consent to XXXX, EQUIFAX, XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX, & XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, whether verbal, nonverbal, written or otherwise implied and I Opt. Out per 15 U.S. Code 6802, effective immediately and indefinitely. 15 U.S. Code 6802 ( b ) ( 1 ) states A financial institution may not disclose nonpublic personal information to a nonaffiliated third party unless 6802 ( b ) ( 1 ) ( B ) the consumer is given the opportunity, before the time that such information is initially disclosed, to direct that such information not be disclosed to such third party ; and 6802 ( b ) ( 1 ) ( C ) the consumer is given an explanation of how the consumer can exercise that nondisclosure option. XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX, & XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX have left me unaware of any nondisclosure options I may have as a consumer, nor have they provided me with any correspondence explaining how I might exercise these rights. And in further unfair treatment of my personal information and poor practice the financial institutions are in violation of 15 U.S. Code 1681c ( a ) ( 5 ). 15 U.S. Code 1681c ( a ) states Except as authorized under subsection ( b ), no consumer reporting agency may make any consumer report containing any of the following items of information : ( 5 ) Any other adverse item of information, other than records of convictions of crimes which antedates the report by more than seven years. These accounts are clearly an adverse item which they are again reporting without my permission, and Congress has deemed disclosure without my consent is against the law. Further supporting my claim, 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2 ( a ) ( 1 ) A states A person shall not furnish any information relating to a consumer to any consumer reporting agency if the person knows or has reasonable cause to believe that the information is inaccurate. And 15 U.S. Code 1681e states Every consumer reporting agency shall maintain reasonable procedures designed to avoid violations of section 1681c of this title and to limit the furnishing of consumer reports to the purposes listed under section 1681b of this title. XXXX, Equifax, & XXXX have clearly abandoned all fair practice guidelines and have failed to maintain reasonable procedures. I further exercise my right to opt out per 12 C.F.R 1016.7 which states A consumer may exercise the right to opt out at any time. Effective immediately and indefinitely I am opting out of your reporting services. 

Privacy Act of 1974 ( 5 U.S. Code 522a ( 4 ) the term record means any item, collection, or grouping of information about an individual that is maintained by an agency, including, but not limited to, his education, financial transactions, medical history, and criminal or employment history and that contains his name, or the identifying number, symbol, or other identifying particular assigned to the individual, such as a finger or voice print or a photograph, this obviously includes consumer reports.

( b ) Conditions of Disclosure No agency shall disclose any record which is contained in a system of records by any means of communication to any person, or to another agency, except pursuant to a written request by, or with the prior written consent of, the individual to whom the record pertains, unless disclosure of the record would be : ( 12 ) to a consumer reporting agency in accordance with section 3711e of title 31. 
IT IS CLEAR THAT DISCLOSURE REQUIRES MY PRIOR WRITTEN INSTRUCTIONS TO DO SO, AND AS THE CONSUMER, I HAVE NOT GIVEN ANY WRITTEN INSTRUCTIONS- I DO NOT GIVE ANY AUTHORIZATION WHATSOEVER. 
XXXX, EQUIFAX, XXXX AND XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX, & XXXX FXXXX XXXX XXXX HAVE VIOLATED MY RIGHTS NOT ONLY PER THE FAIR CREDIT REPORTING ACT BUT ALSO THE PRIVACY ACT OF 1974. I am not asking for a pristine consumer report, but an ACCCURATE AND FAIR CONSUMER REPORT.",None,"EQUIFAX, INC.",NY,14225,None,Consent provided,Web,03/17/24,Closed with non-monetary relief,Yes,N/A,8565950
03/13/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"As a vigilant guardian of my own privacy, I invoke the power vested in me by the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) and the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ) to address the violations concerning my personal information. The FCRA, under 15 U.S.C. 1681 section 602 ( a ), mandates consumer reporting agencies to handle their significant responsibilities with fairness, impartiality, and respect for my right to privacy. XXXX and Experian, as consumer reporting agencies, must heed this directive. I assert my right to ensure that my private information remains confidential, a right that is fortified by 15 U.S.C. 6801, which clearly states that financial institutions have an ongoing obligation to honor the privacy of their customers. The entity that furnishes information to credit agencies falls under this definition and thus, is bound by these laws. Furthermore, 15 U.S.C. 1681 section 604 ( a ) ( 2 ) specifies that a consumer reporting agency may furnish a report only with my written consent, which neither XXXX, Experian, XXXX nor the furnisher of information has obtained from me. I hereby revoke any form of consent previously granted, whether verbal, non-verbal, written, implied, or otherwise. The law is clear in 15 U.S.C. 6802 ( b ) that a financial institution can not disclose my nonpublic personal information to a nonaffiliated third party without informing me of my right to nondisclosure, a right that was never presented to me. Moreover, 15 U.S.C. 1681C ( a ) ( 5 ) prohibits the reporting of any adverse information older than seven years, which includes the account in question. This is a violation of my legal rights, as there is no permission granted on my part for such reporting. The accuracy of information is paramount, as stated in 15 U.S.C. 1681s2 ( A ) ( 1 ) ( A ), which forbids the furnishing of information to consumer reporting agencies if it is known or suspected to be inaccurate. XXXX and Experians failure to maintain reasonable procedures, as required by 15 U.S.C. 1681e, to prevent such violations further compounds the issue. Lastly, in accordance with 12 CFR 1016.7, I exercise my right to opt out of your reporting services at this time. My privacy is not a commodity for trade, and I demand the respect and confidentiality that is rightfully mine. Please be advised that this notice of my rights has been issued to you multiple times and has been met with disregard. This persistent neglect is not only disrespectful but also a clear breach of the legal obligations outlined above. I insist on immediate compliance with these regulations, and any further inaction will be met with the necessary legal recourse to protect my rights.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,Experian Information Solutions Inc.,NY,10462,None,Consent provided,Web,03/13/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8542880
03/13/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Account information incorrect,"To Whom It May Concern, I am writing to file a formal complaint regarding the handling of an issue by Bank of America and XXXX, which has led to significant financial and emotional distress on my part. 

Approximately XXXX months ago, I initiated a claim with Bank of America due to an accounting error on their part. They erroneously charged my credit card account several XXXX dollars due to a misunderstanding. Despite numerous attempts to rectify the situation through extensive communication with Bank of America, the issue persisted unresolved for an extended period. 

Frustrated by the lack of progress and the financial impact caused by Bank of America 's error, I escalated the matter by opening a claim with the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau ( CFPB ). Subsequently, the issue was finally resolved after intervention from the CFPB, and I believed the matter to be settled. 

However, on XX/XX/2024, I discovered a drastic drop of approximately XXXX points in my credit score. Upon further investigation, I found that this decline was attributed to an "" overdue balance '' on my account, which was, in fact, the same erroneous charge that Bank of America had previously acknowledged and resolved. Despite being assured of resolution, Bank of America 's negligence persisted, causing severe harm to my creditworthiness. 

In an attempt to rectify the situation, I promptly initiated a dispute with XXXX, one of the major credit reporting agencies, providing all necessary documentation to support my claim. Despite my efforts, XXXX XXXX response, received on XX/XX/2024, failed to address the inaccuracies in my credit report. The reported "" 30 days overdue '' payment for XXXX continues to unjustly tarnish my credit history, solely due to Bank of America 's repeated errors. 

The relentless pursuit of resolution has taken an immense toll on my time, energy, and emotional well-being. Despite investing countless hours in communication with unresponsive and unhelpful customer service representatives, the issue remains unresolved. The incompetence and lack of professionalism exhibited by Bank of America have pushed me to my limits, and I am left with no choice but to seek intervention from the CFPB. 

I urge the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau to intervene promptly and compel Bank of America and XXXX to rectify the inaccuracies in my credit report. Furthermore, I request that appropriate measures be taken to compensate for the damages incurred as a result of their negligence. Failure to address this matter satisfactorily will leave me with no option but to pursue legal recourse, including seeking legal counsel to pursue further action against both companies. 

I appreciate your attention to this matter and look forward to a swift resolution. 

Sincerely, XXXX XXXX Attached documents contain : The XXXX dispute results, the original letter I had sent to XXXX regarding the dispute, as well as the original dispute I had submitted to the CFPB to resolve the initial issue of an accounting error of Bank of America 's.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"BANK OF AMERICA, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION",NY,10026,None,Consent provided,Web,03/13/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8526938
