05/27/22,Checking or savings account,Checking account,Managing an account,Funds not handled or disbursed as instructed,"In XX/XX/2021, two checks from my bank account were intercepted in the mail. The original information was erased or whited out, and the checks were forged with different amounts to two separate recipients and deposited in fraudulent accounts in XXXX XXXX. The checks were # XXXX for {$16000.00} withdrawn on XX/XX/2021 and # XXXX for {$9500.00} withdrawn on XX/XX/2021. I was traveling in XXXX and XXXX and don't believe I received my XXXX statement that would have shown the deposit of check # XXXX as a result of my mail being held. I filed a claim ( claim # XXXX ) with my bank, Chase on XX/XX/2021. I also filed a police report with XXXX XXXX ( Complaint # XXXX ). 
After several months, Chase reimbursed me {$9500.00} for check # XXXX, but refused to reimburse the {$16000.00} because my claim was more than 35 days past the date that the check posted. From the Chase side, both checks were several times larger than any check I had ever written before, the handwriting was not mine, and the out-of-state recipients had no interaction with my account in the past. There were no fraud alerts or attempts to contact me. From the the XXXX XXXX side, the fact that these were deposited into two separate fraudulent accounts in the same bank demonstrates negligently inadequate KYC controls. 

I am in disbelief that this can happen in an American banking system ... .that if you don't catch something in 35 days, that the money is gone ... that there would be no flagging or fraud alerts despite the size of the checks, the different handwriting and the likely suspicious activity on the accounts that the checks were deposited into.",None,JPMORGAN CHASE & CO.,NY,10028,None,Consent provided,Web,05/27/22,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,5618301
