03/12/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"On XX/XX/XXXX I wrote to XXXX, the Executive VP at TransUnion stating that the credit reporting agency violated my rights as an American consumer by sharing my personal, non public information without my consent. 15 USC 1681 ( b ) Permissible purpose of consumer reports, clearly states that any consumer reporting agency may furnish a consumer report under the following circumstances and No Other. They need your written consent to add anything to consumer report if you did not give this authorization. There is also an exclusion, which states that reporting transactional data is, in fact, illegal. 15 USC 6802 ( a ) ( b ) Obligations with respect to Personal Information and 12 CFR 1022.3 defines Identity Theft as fraud committed or attempted using the identifying information of another person without authorization. Anything added to your report without written Consent is considered identity theft. 15 USC 1681 ( n ) Civil liability for willful noncompliance and in general ; Any person who willfully fails to comply with any requirement imposed under this subchapter with respect to any consumer is liable to that consumer in the amount equal to the sum of. ( A ) and ( B ). And finally the Privacy Act of XXXX ( b ) Conditions of Disclosure.

On top of that, there are multiple inquiries on my credit report that should not be there. 15 USC 1681 ( b ) Permissible Purposes clearly states that if the consumer does not have an open account with the lender that inquired, they have to remove the inquiry OR send the documentation that they used to verify the open account. I also requested that they remove any address listed on my report besides the one listed on my letterhead, for they are Non-Mailable addresses and should not be there. In my letter I clearly stated that This information was obtained unlawfully and failure to act would make them liable. This letter was sent in XXXX, it is now XXXX, and in their response they claimed that they can basically do whatever they want and no changes were made to my report. This gross negligence has caused immense damage to my quality of life and has hindered my ability to pursue happiness. Willful non-compliance can easily be proven with record of the original letter, their written response, and their failure to comply. I request IMMEDIATE action to remedy this violation of my consumer rights.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"TRANSUNION INTERMEDIATE HOLDINGS, INC.",NY,11570,None,Consent provided,Web,03/12/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8532028
