05/08/24,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Old information reappears or never goes away,"I am writing to file a formal complaint with the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau ( CFPB ) regarding a 7-year-old charged-off Capital One credit card that continues to appear on my credit report. This account, with partial number XXXX, was opened on XX/XX/XXXX. Despite the Fair Credit Reporting Act 's ( FCRA ) seven-year limit for reporting negative information, this charged-off account remains on my credit report, causing significant harm to my credit score and overall financial opportunities. 

Legal Grounds for Complaint Fair Credit Reporting Act ( 15 USC 1681 ) The FCRA stipulates that negative information like charge-offs should be removed after seven years from the date of the original delinquency. Since this charge-off is over seven years old, it should no longer be reported.

Fair Credit Billing Act ( 15 USC 1666b ) The Fair Credit Billing Act requires creditors to promptly correct billing errors and discrepancies. The continued reporting of this charge-off could indicate improper handling of account information. 
Privacy Act of 1974 This act ensures consumer privacy and control over personal information. The continued presence of a charged-off account beyond its permissible reporting period raises privacy concerns.

Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( 15 USC 1692 ) The FDCPA prohibits unfair or deceptive practices in debt collection. The ongoing report of a charged-off debt may suggest improper debt collection practices. 
IRS Regulations on Discharged Debt According to IRS rules, a discharged or forgiven debt should not be reported on a credit report. Additionally, the absence of a 1099-C form, which confirms a discharged debt for tax purposes, raises questions about the accuracy of this charge-off.

Requested Actions I request the following from the CFPB : Immediate Removal of the Charge-Off Given that the charge-off is over seven years old, it should be removed from my credit report. 
Investigation into Compliance with IRS Regulations Examine whether Capital One issued a 1099-C, as required by IRS regulations , for this charged-off debt.

Review of Account Handling Investigate whether Capital One violated any provisions of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, Fair Credit Billing Act, or Fair Debt Collection Practices Act. 
I request a prompt response and appropriate action to correct this situation. If further information or documentation is required, I am prepared to provide it upon request. Thank you for your attention to this matter, and I look forward to hearing from you soon. 

Sincerely, XXXX XXXX XXXX",None,CAPITAL ONE FINANCIAL CORPORATION,NY,11225,None,Consent provided,Web,05/08/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8958786
