05/05/24,Debt collection,Credit card debt,Took or threatened to take negative or legal action,Threatened or suggested your credit would be damaged,"I am facing financial harassment and severe damage to my credit due to actions by the XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, located at XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX, XXXX, UT XXXX, regarding an alleged debt from XX/XX/XXXX. They are deliberately communicating with credit reporting agencies to harm my credit, which is negatively impacting my low-income family 's ability to thrive, particularly as a XXXX individual. 

It's important to note that federal laws, including the XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX XXXX and the XXXX XXXX, provide protections for qualified individuals with XXXX. In addition, under New York law, the statute of limitations for consumer debt is 3 years. This means that creditors or debt collectors have a maximum of 3 years to attempt to collect on an unpaid debt or initiate legal action. Once this time period has lapsed, the debt is considered time-barred. 

The New York Consumer Credit Fairness Act ( CCFA ), which came into effect on XX/XX/XXXX, governs such limitations. It applies to both original creditors like credit card companies and debt collectors. Amendments to the New York Civil Practice Laws reinforce these regulations. Importantly, once the statute of limitations has expired, it can not be reinstated or extended, even if payments are made or acknowledgment of the debt is provided. 

For debt buyers and collection agencies to pursue legal action, they must demonstrate ownership of the debt and the right to sue for its collection. This entails providing a complete chain of custody for the debt, even if it has been transferred through multiple entities. Failure to prove ownership will result in the dismissal of the case. I want the CFPB to publish this description on consumerfinance.gov so that others can learn from my horrific experience.",None,Credit Corp Solutions Inc.,NY,11420,None,Consent provided,Web,05/05/24,Closed with explanation,Yes,N/A,8933644
